--- body ---
## Context

The current tree interleaves durable doctrine with Claude syntax and host policy. Moving
files before naming the boundary would merely relocate coupling. This ticket defines what
is canonical, what is an adapter concern, and which current Claude behaviors must survive.

## Relevant files

- `README.md` — current product model and Five Claws
- `CLAUDE.md` — current always-loaded entry point
- `rules/` — mostly portable doctrine with provider references
- `skills/` — workflows plus Claude frontmatter and invocation syntax
- `settings.shared.json` — hooks, permissions and host-specific settings
- `scripts/on-start.sh` — current session-start behavior

## Actions

1. Inventory provider tokens, absolute paths, lifecycle events and runtime assumptions.
2. Write `core/ARCHITECTURE.md` defining core, adapter, profile, runtime-state and generated
   ownership; include a rule for every ambiguous existing directory.
3. Record a behavioral baseline for session start, one simple skill, one orchestrating
   skill, one safety hook and one verification gate.
4. Define canonical identifiers for rules, skills, roles, capabilities and lifecycle events.
5. Mark non-goals: no lowest-common-denominator core, no cloud control of local systemd,
   and no manual duplicate workflow bodies.

## Test

- First add a failing architecture check that detects forbidden provider names in a small
  neutral-core fixture; make it pass with the documented boundary and allowlisted adapter
  references.

## Verification

- [ ] Every current top-level directory has exactly one ownership class
- [ ] Baseline covers both instructions and executable enforcement
- [ ] The core vocabulary contains no Claude- or Codex-specific invocation syntax
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Invariants

- This ticket documents and tests the boundary; it does not migrate the complete harness.
- Existing Claude installation paths and behavior remain unchanged.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Neutral-core architecture and canonical identifier rules are committed
- [ ] Behavioral baseline is executable or machine-checkable
- [ ] Follow-on tickets can identify their source and generated outputs unambiguously
